- venue: Journal of Physics, Conference Series
- citation count: 0
[method]
The paper used a probabilistic agent-based simulation of individual bats foraging in a habitat with a constant daily resource rate. Each bat had a foraging efficiency randomly assigned between 0.5 and 1, and per-capita resource availability was inversely proportional to population size. A successful forager stored three food units; an unsuccessful forager lost one unit and died if its store reached zero, approximating death after three consecutive foraging failures. Each bat reproduced once per year at a random time, and offspring inherited the mother's efficiency. In the sharing variant, bats with at least three stored units were donors and bats with one stored unit were needy; each needy bat received one unit from a randomly chosen donor when needy bats were fewer than donors, otherwise each donor chose one needy bat. Sharing could be made probabilistic with a cooperativity parameter w, and individual storage capacity c was varied from 3 to 12. Sharing and non-sharing populations were initialized with the same number of individuals, same efficiencies, and same external resource input; transients were discarded after stabilization. The simulation measured stable population size, total stored food, per-capita resource use, competitive replacement between pre-classified sharing and non-sharing groups, and average age and age at death. This is related to the proposed method and research plan because it is an individual-based vertebrate cooperation model that quantifies helping/resource-sharing behavior, group/population size, and survival/age outcomes, but it does not implement the proposed division-of-labor tasks, task specialization, temporal polyethism, dispersal, relatedness, or direct-versus-indirect fitness variants.
[results]
For the baseline parameter set, the stable population with resource sharing was 417 individuals, compared with 277 in the non-sharing population, an approximately 50% increase in sustainable population size. The sharing population assimilated about 11% more total daily resources, and its per-individual resource requirement was about one-fourth lower. In a competition experiment with equal initial numbers of sharing and non-sharing individuals, no transfers between types, and no defectors, sharing individuals eventually took over the population. Increasing the cooperativity parameter w produced nonlinear benefits: substantial group benefits appeared only after w exceeded 60%, after which benefits increased sharply toward a maximum. Increasing storage capacity c from 3 to 12 reduced stable group size in both sharing and non-sharing populations; the sharing-to-non-sharing population ratio was nonmonotonic with c, while total stored resources showed the inverse pattern. Average age and average age at death were statistically similar between sharing and non-sharing populations and were not affected by resource availability or initial population size. The authors also reported a theoretical expression for the average waiting time to three consecutive unsuccessful forages and stated that, with a 10% foraging-failure rate, this event would occur after more than three years, compared with an average lifespan of about 10 years. These outcomes are relevant to the proposed research plan as a baseline for how a simple helping rule can alter survival and demographic carrying capacity in a vertebrate group, but they do not test task-specific fitness costs or the evolution of division of labor.
[context]
The simulations were implemented in NetLogo, with code made available for reproduction; no hardware specifications or runtime details were reported. Key conditions included a constant habitat resource rate, equal initial population sizes and foraging efficiencies for sharing and non-sharing comparisons, annual reproduction, maternal inheritance of foraging efficiency, death only by resource depletion, and no dispersal, no relatedness structure, and no task specialization. The sharing rule was all-or-nothing transfer of one food unit, with optional probabilistic sharing controlled by w and storage capacity c varied from 3 to 12. In the competitive variant, sharing and non-sharing individuals were kept distinct, with no resource transfers between types and no defection. These constraints make the paper a simplified demographic model of cooperative resource sharing rather than a full division-of-labor model, so its relevance to the proposed method is limited to shared outcome measures such as helping behavior, group size, and survival/age, while it lacks the proposed measures of task specialization, age-dependent task switching, dispersal, within-group relatedness, and direct/indirect fitness decomposition.
[end related work 1]

